WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.180
Welcome to the show.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:05.871
So, um, we have been having some, uh, complexities
for [inaudible].

00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:06.871
We're

00:00:06.871 --> 00:00:11.650
figuring out exactly what should be said in
this segment because of the schedule for filming

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:16.769
this week and the very, very long impeachment
hearings that have been happening in the house

00:00:16.769 --> 00:00:17.960
judiciary committee.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:25.099
Uh, we are having to tape this segment before
we actually know the full outcome and schedule

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:26.670
of what has been taking place.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:32.360
So what I am saying right now may be outdated
or it may have already happened or it may

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.760
be about to happen.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.211
Uh, but I want to kind of give an overview
of what is going to happen with what is the

00:00:38.211 --> 00:00:43.010
impeachment of Donald Trump by the house of
representatives currently with a democratic

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.010
majority.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:50.530
Uh, Thursday, very, very long hearings in
the house judiciary committee made up of 41

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:58.830
members in total, 24 Democrats, 17 Republicans
that body, uh, after debating on and on and

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:03.260
on, a variety of amendments, which of course
never had an opportunity to pass, but merely

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.499
served as a jumping off point for grand standing.

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:14.219
And for the repetition of what at this point
is very exhaustingly the same talking points

00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:19.840
over and over and over again, monopolizing
news television for the entire day.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.630
The house judiciary will or already did vote
among along party lines.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:32.530
Uh, yes on impeachment of Donald Trump sending
the vote to the full house in the house.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:40.000
They will or may have already voted basically
along party lines to impeach Donald Trump,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.850
the president of the United States.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.090
Now, what would be interesting and as of this
filming we don't know, but that I would be

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:52.999
curious to see, would be, are there any democratic
members of the house that vote against impeachment

00:01:52.999 --> 00:01:56.869
or are there any Republican members of the
house that vote for the impeachment of Donald

00:01:56.869 --> 00:01:57.869
Trump?

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:02.649
For example, we had heard at different points
in time that there's a, um, uh, where she

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:08.369
from Hawaii Congresswoman tools he gathered
who at least one point was not in favor of

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:09.369
impeachment.

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:10.369
How does she vote, if at all?

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:12.560
She's been missing from a lot of votes as
well.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.810
Are there any, uh, individuals that cross
party lines?

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:21.150
What then will happen is that the Senate will
have a trial and it appears as though that

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.920
is going to be in January.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.890
Now everybody has different interests in how
this trial, um, uh, is going to go or may

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:28.890
go.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.349
The trial will be organized by the Senate
majority leader.

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:38.010
Remember, despite the house being taken over
by Democrats in November, 2018 sworn in January

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.900
of this year, the Senate is still controlled
by Republicans and that means that the Senate

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.200
majority leader Mitch McConnell gets to set
a lot of the parameters and procedure of how

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.560
that impeachment trial goes.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:57.780
We in looking at the tentative 2020 Senate
calendar, we see that January has been left

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.299
blank at least as of this filming with the
expectation that this is when the impeachment

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:03.299
trial is going to take.

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:05.940
I have been asked how long could that impeachment
trial go?

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.549
It could be a week to 10 days.

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:10.660
It could be the majority of January.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:11.680
We just don't know yet.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.810
Donald Trump has reportedly expressed an interest
in it going a very long time.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:21.340
He believes maybe rightly, maybe wrongly,
that along impeachment trial would be good

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.700
for him.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.019
Uh, however, presiding over the impeachment
trial will be the chief justice of the Supreme

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:33.200
court of the United States, which is of course,
John Roberts, a conservative justice, not

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.799
selected by president Donald Trump, but a
conservative justice nonetheless.

00:03:37.799 --> 00:03:41.769
So then the question is, uh, what is going
to happen in the Senate?

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:46.660
And I will give you some of the counterpoints
to this, but the most likely scenario, I am

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:51.140
not a betting man, but if I were a betting
man, I would imagine that it is going to be

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:56.340
a, uh, an acquittal of Donald Trump in the
Senate.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.480
Why do I say that?

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:03.930
Well, so far we have seen no interest from
Republican senators to seriously even consider

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:09.390
the possibility of analyzing what it is that
has taken place and being open to voting to

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.620
convict Donald Trump a priori.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:17.190
We have seen Republicans dismiss the case
for impeachment, uh, save, you know, these

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.289
little, uh, blips that we could maybe talk
about.

00:04:21.289 --> 00:04:26.530
Uh, currently the numbers are against Democrats
in the sense that there are 45 democratic

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:27.530
senators right now.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:33.680
There are two independent senators, Bernie
Sanders and Angus King who caucus with Democrats.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.169
And we could say that's 47 votes right there.

00:04:37.169 --> 00:04:42.330
Now there is a question as to will every,
you know, the Senate has some very centrist

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:47.449
or we could even call them conservative Democrats,
Joe mansion for example, will Joe mansion

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:49.080
vote to convict Donald Trump on impeachment?

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.250
I'm not sure.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:56.120
So we may even not have all 47 Democrats to
count on on the other side because you need

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.550
a two thirds vote to convict on impeachment.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.080
Two-thirds being with a hundred senators,
it's 67.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:09.259
Uh, that means you need to pick up 20 Republican
senators who are, forget about, interested

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:12.380
in convicting, even open to that idea.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:13.819
I don't think they exist.

00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:15.740
Uh, and let's go through down.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:18.539
Let's go down that path now in a couple of
different ways.

00:05:18.539 --> 00:05:23.290
First of all, Republican senators are just
going to do what's politically advantageous.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:28.680
And that means in terms of their reelection,
in terms of balancing that with maintaining

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.690
a, a position in Donald Trump's good graces,
et cetera.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:38.990
Uh, if they put their finger to the wind,
and we know from polling that nearly two thirds

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:44.670
of Republican voters said, not only am I not
against Trump right now, there's nothing Trump

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.889
could even hypothetically do that would turn
me against him.

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:52.979
When two thirds of Republican voters, 62%,
almost two thirds say that, why would a Republican

00:05:52.979 --> 00:06:00.139
Senator vote to convict when Republican voters
would likely punish them for that decision?

00:06:00.139 --> 00:06:06.400
The caveat to this is that we've heard, you
know, a former Senator Jeff flake said he

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.349
thinks potentially two dozen or more, I think
30 was the number of Republican senators might

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:14.550
be willing to convict Trump on impeachment.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.039
What, who was he talking about?

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:17.039
What is he talking about?

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:18.039
We don't know.

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:22.020
More recently, democratic Senator Chris Murphy
from Connecticut said that he can count them

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.069
on one hand, but they exist.

00:06:24.069 --> 00:06:26.500
Republicans who he believes would be willing
to convict Donald Trump.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.699
And we went through the list always on that
list is Senator Susan Collins from Maine.

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:36.690
Although despite her vocalizing sometimes
some opposition to the Republican orthodoxy,

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:41.039
in the end, she usually ends up being a tool
of perpetuating the Republican orthodoxy,

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:44.190
the Brett Kavanaugh hearings being an example
of that.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:47.889
Anytime we talk about there is a Republican
who might go the other way.

00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:51.660
Lisa Murkowski's name comes up from Alaska.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.900
Mitt Romney, who is now a Senator in Utah,
has had some public disagreements with Donald

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:56.900
Trump.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:00.110
They were friends and they had turtle soup
or whatever it was, frog legs and turtle soup.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:03.479
Then they were not friends and Trump ridiculed
him, whatever.

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:08.919
Romney has talked, uh, before, uh, when he,
when I believe Romney was asked about Republican

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:13.870
Congressman Justin Amash a mash, I forget
how he pronounces his name, Justin mash saying,

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:15.500
Hey, listen, what Trump's doing is wrong.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.750
We've got to impeach.

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:20.530
Romney said, I respect what he's doing or
what he's doing is brave or courageous or

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:21.889
something like that.

00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:23.680
But that's not for me.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.180
So even though Romney's name is being floated
as a possibility, is that likely?

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.410
I don't know how Republicans get 20 senators
to say, we're going to vote to convict.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.289
So then let's carry that out.

00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:42.140
January goes by and Donald Trump is acquitted
in a Senate impeachment trial.

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:48.370
Then what Trump convicts a Trump rather a
campaigns on vindication.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:49.490
Does that work?

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:55.289
Certainly non acquittal, despite not necessarily
being vindication because it's going to be

00:07:55.289 --> 00:07:58.400
a party line thing, it likely will reinvigorate.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.639
Uh, some voters who voted for Trump in 2016
are not thrilled with Trump's performance

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:05.860
since being inaugurated.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:09.669
Maybe would have stayed home, probably would
not vote for a Democrat in 2020, but maybe

00:08:09.669 --> 00:08:11.240
it would've just stayed home.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:15.420
They may say, wow, this guy, they've gone
after this guy on Russia now.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.979
They went after him on Ukraine and he was
acquitted.

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:21.819
I've got to get out and support him because
after all this, I don't want a Democrat getting

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:22.819
in.

00:08:22.819 --> 00:08:28.030
This guy's been through the ringer that is
likely to invigorates that, that ideology

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.759
in at least some 2016 Trump voters.

00:08:30.759 --> 00:08:39.310
Then it becomes Ken use what is obviously
going to be a, an a priori, a non conviction

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:40.620
by a Senate.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:45.280
Republicans, can they use that to say, guys,
we've got to get out and vote.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.570
We, we went through the hearings, we looked
at the evidence.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:54.269
We did what we could because we control the
house, but the Senate simply will not see

00:08:54.269 --> 00:08:55.269
reason.

00:08:55.269 --> 00:08:56.600
We now need to vote.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.579
It will then be the end of January or early
February.

00:08:59.579 --> 00:09:01.769
It will only be nine months from that election.

00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:06.250
We will start to have the Iowa caucuses and
then the New Hampshire primary leading up

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:10.930
to super Tuesday and all of those, the timing
may, and I just don't know.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:17.700
The timing may really inspire a lot of uh,
left winners, particularly those who maybe

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.630
have not voted because remember half the country
doesn't vote.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:25.709
It may inspire them to get involved and say,
this can't be, I've been sitting on the sidelines.

00:09:25.709 --> 00:09:29.630
I gave them the opportunity to get this guy
out with impeachment for what he's done.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.670
I need to now go and vote in November.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.519
Will, that enthusiasm lasts for nine months.

00:09:34.519 --> 00:09:36.149
I don't know.

00:09:36.149 --> 00:09:39.850
Voting enthusiasm is a fickle and difficult
to predict thing.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.079
So that's my assessment of the next.

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:43.690
What is roughly going to be 11 months.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:48.140
Again, we had to film this before the house
actually having that full vote.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:51.400
It may not yet have happened when you listen
to this, it may already have happened when

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:54.830
you watch this, but we will pick this story
up on Monday.

